const [px, py] = vec(ctx.input("position"));
const [ax, ay] = vec(ctx.input("anchor"));
const [w, h] = vec(ctx.input("size")).map((v) => Math.max(0, v));
if (w === 0 || h === 0) return ctx.output("shape", { path: "" });
const r = num(ctx.input("cornerRadius"));
const perCorner = vec(ctx.input("cornerRadii"));       // [topLeft, topRight, bottomRight, bottomLeft]
const radii = ctx.isConnected("cornerRadii") || perCorner.some((v) => v > 0) ? perCorner : [r, r, r, r];
const smoothing = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, num(ctx.input("cornerSmoothing"))));
const left = px - ax * w;
const top = py - ay * h;
ctx.output("shape", { path: squirclePath(left, top, w, h, radii, smoothing) });
```

- Reading inputs: `num(v)` and `vec(v)` return finite numbers. A non-finite value (NaN, ±Infinity) reads as 0, and the patch warns once per restart with `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.
- Geometry: `squirclePath(x, y, w, h, radii, smoothing)` from `@sonobe/engine` is the DOM-free function the renderer also uses for Rectangle layer corners, so a Shape layer fed by this patch matches a Rectangle layer with the same size, radii, and smoothing pixel for pixel.
- What `squirclePath` does: negative radii read as 0. Each corner shares the length of its two edges with the neighboring corners in proportion to their radii and is capped by that room, so a uniform radius is capped at half the shorter side (pill ends). Smoothing 0 draws circular arcs; above 0 each arc shortens and blends into its edges with two cubic Béziers (Figma's squircle construction). Its numbers are rounded to 3 decimals, and its text is used as is.
- Winding and trimming: the path starts on the top edge where the top-right corner's curve begins and runs clockwise on screen (y down), so Stroke Start and Stroke End trim from near the top-right corner. Shape Union expects this clockwise winding.
- Corner Radii precedence: while `cornerRadii` is connected it always wins, even when every value is 0, so an animation passing through [0, 0, 0, 0] doesn't jump back to Corner Radius. Unconnected, it wins when any value is above 0.
- Coordinates: layer-local points of the Shape layer the output drives (`ShapeValue.path`), with the origin at its top-left and y down. The renderer draws the path without clipping it to the layer's size.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. The output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. An empty loop gives an empty output. A Shape layer bound to a loop of shapes replicates, one instance per item.
- Edge cases: negative size components read as 0, and a width or height of 0 outputs the empty shape silently. The empty shape is `{ path: "" }`; the Shape layer draws nothing for it. Coordinates are clamped to ±1,000,000.
- Hit testing: touches land on the Shape layer's box (its position and size), not on the path. Parts of the path outside that box still draw but don't receive touches.
- Coming from Origami: the serialized id is `builtin.shape.roundRect` (the docs slug is lowercase). The Radius port imports as `cornerRadius`. Origami doesn't document how it limits large radii or whether Position is the center; Sonobe caps radii as above and uses the center, consistent with Circle. Importers translate Position from Origami's parent-center coordinates. The advanced ports have no Origami equivalents and import as their defaults.

