Each frame, for this loop index:
```
let list = ctx.input("points");
if (typeof list === "string") list = tryParseJson(list);          // undefined when the text isn't JSON
let pts = Array.isArray(list) ? list.slice(0, 10000).map(readPoint).filter(Boolean) : [];
if (pts.length < 2) pts = [vec(ctx.input("startPoint")), vec(ctx.input("endPoint"))];
const closed = ctx.input("closed") === true;
const s = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, num(ctx.input("smoothing"))));
const n = pts.length;
const at = (i) => (closed ? pts[(i + n) % n] : pts[Math.min(n - 1, Math.max(0, i))]);
const parts = [cmd("M", ...pts[0])];
const segments = closed ? n : n - 1;
for (let i = 0; i < segments; i++) {
  const p0 = at(i);
  const p1 = at(i + 1);
  if (s === 0) { parts.push(cmd("L", ...p1)); continue; }
  // Catmull-Rom through the neighbors, written as a cubic Bézier.
  const prev = at(i - 1);
  const next = at(i + 2);
  const c1 = [p0[0] + ((p1[0] - prev[0]) * s) / 6, p0[1] + ((p1[1] - prev[1]) * s) / 6];
  const c2 = [p1[0] - ((next[0] - p0[0]) * s) / 6, p1[1] - ((next[1] - p0[1]) * s) / 6];
  parts.push(cmd("C", ...c1, ...c2, ...p1));
}
if (closed) parts.push("Z");
ctx.output("shape", { path: parts.join(" ") });
```

- Reading inputs: `num(v)` and `vec(v)` return finite numbers. A non-finite value (NaN, ±Infinity) reads as 0, and the patch warns once per restart with `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.
- `readPoint(item)` accepts `[x, y]` arrays (extra items are ignored) and objects with `x` and `y`. Each coordinate must be a finite number or a numeric string such as `"12.5"`; anything else makes the item invalid and returns `null`.
- Path text: `cmd(letter, ...numbers)` writes the command letter followed by its numbers separated by single spaces, and commands are joined with one space (`M50 0 A50 50 0 0 1 100 50`). `f(n)` clamps to ±1,000,000, rounds to 3 decimals, and writes `-0` as `0`, so there's never exponent notation. The same inputs always give byte-identical text, which keeps traces and downstream `ctx.changed` stable.
- Points precedence: when `points` yields 2 or more valid points, Start Point and End Point are ignored. Invalid items are skipped. Skipped items, text that isn't JSON, a non-array value other than `null` or `[]`, or a list longer than 10,000 items each warn once per restart.
- Smoothing: `s = 0` draws straight `L` segments. Above 0, each segment is a uniform Catmull-Rom curve scaled by `s`; `s = 1` is the standard curve. Open lines reuse the end points as their own neighbors, so a two-point line stays straight at any smoothing. Closed lines wrap neighbors around.
- Winding and trimming: the path starts at the first point and follows the list order, so Stroke Start and Stroke End trim from the first point.
- Fill: SVG fills an open path as if a straight edge joined its last point to its first. A Shape layer with an opaque Color therefore fills the area under an open chart line. This is the layer's behavior; the patch doesn't change it.
- Edge cases: when every point is the same, the zero-length path is still output; with round line caps, a stroke draws a dot. Coordinates are clamped to ±1,000,000.
- Coordinates: layer-local points of the Shape layer the output drives (`ShapeValue.path`), with the origin at its top-left and y down. The renderer draws the path without clipping it to the layer's size.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. The output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. `points` is one JSON value per index, so a loop of points wired into Points gives single points per index, which fall back to Start Point and End Point. Convert the loop with Loop to Array first.
- Hit testing: touches land on the Shape layer's box (its position and size), not on the path. Parts of the path outside that box still draw but don't receive touches.
- Coming from Origami: Origami has no line patch; people draw lines with thin rectangles or JSON to Shape. Importers never produce this patch.

Stateless: outputs depend only on the current inputs. Per loop index, cache the last `(pathData, viewBox, size)` and its result, and reparse only when one of them changes; the cache never changes outputs.

Each frame, for this loop index:
```
let text = String(ctx.input("pathData") ?? "");
let viewBox = vec(ctx.input("viewBox"));                   // [x, y, width, height]
const [w, h] = vec(ctx.input("size")).map((v) => Math.max(0, v));
const problems = [];
if (text.length > 1000000) return done([], ["Path data is longer than 1,000,000 characters."]);
if (text.trimStart().startsWith("<")) {
  const svg = readSvgMarkup(text);                         // { paths: string[], viewBox: number[] | null, problems: string[] }
  text = svg.paths.join(" ");
  if (!(viewBox[2] > 0 && viewBox[3] > 0) && svg.viewBox) viewBox = svg.viewBox;
  problems.push(...svg.problems);
}
const { segments, syntaxError } = parsePath(text);         // absolute M, L, C, Q, A, Z
if (syntaxError) problems.unshift(syntaxError);
const [vx, vy, vw, vh] = viewBox;
if (vw > 0 && vh > 0) {
  if (w === 0 || h === 0) return done([], problems);
  const s = Math.min(w / vw, h / vh);
  const tx = (w - vw * s) / 2 - vx * s;
  const ty = (h - vh * s) / 2 - vy * s;
  return done(transform(segments, s, tx, ty), problems);   // x → x·s + tx, y → y·s + ty, arc rx and ry × s
}
return done(segments, problems);

// done(segments, problems): output "shape" = { path: format(segments) },
// "error" = problems.length > 0, "errorMessage" = problems.join(" ").
```

- Reading inputs: `num(v)` and `vec(v)` return finite numbers. A non-finite value (NaN, ±Infinity) reads as 0, and the patch warns once per restart with `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`.
- `parsePath(text)` follows the SVG 2 path grammar:
  - Commands `M L H V C S Q T A Z`: upper case is absolute, lower case relative. Commands repeat implicitly when more numbers follow, and extra coordinate pairs after `M`/`m` are treated as `L`/`l`.
  - Numbers take an optional sign, digits, an optional decimal point, and an optional exponent (`1e-3`). Separators (whitespace and at most one comma) are optional where unambiguous: `M1-2.5.5` is `M 1 -2.5 0.5`. Arc flags are single `0` or `1` characters and may run together (`a1 1 0 00 1 1`).
  - The path must start with `M` or `m`; a leading `m` is absolute.
  - Normalize while parsing: relative to absolute; `H` and `V` to `L`; `S` to `C`, reflecting the previous cubic's second control point (or using the current point when the previous command wasn't a cubic); `T` to `Q` the same way. After `Z`, the current point returns to the subpath start. Arcs with `rx` or `ry` of 0 become `L`; other arcs keep their radii, rotation, and flags.
  - On the first syntax error, stop and keep the segments before the failing command, as browsers do with a broken `d`. `syntaxError` reads `Path data stops at character 14: expected a number after "L".` or `Path data stops at character 3: unknown command "X".`, using a 1-based index into the path text.
- `readSvgMarkup(text)` uses a tolerant tag scanner with no DOM and no entity expansion, so it runs headless:
  - `paths`: the `d` attribute of every `<path>` element, in document order, in single or double quotes.
  - `viewBox`: the first `<svg>` element's `viewBox`, four numbers separated by spaces or commas. When that's missing but `width` and `height` are plain numbers (optionally ending in `px`), use `[0, 0, width, height]`; otherwise `null`.
  - `problems`, each added once: other drawable elements (`<circle>`, `<ellipse>`, `<rect>`, `<line>`, `<polyline>`, `<polygon>`, `<text>`, `<image>`, `<use>`) give "Only <path> elements are read; flatten other shapes before copying." Any `transform` attribute gives "Transforms in the SVG are ignored; flatten the artwork before copying." No `<path>` at all gives "The SVG has no <path> elements."
- `format(segments)` writes absolute commands with `cmd` and `f(n)`: the letter followed by numbers separated by single spaces, commands joined with one space. `f(n)` clamps to ±1,000,000, rounds to 3 decimals, and writes `-0` as `0`. Share one path module (parse, normalize, transform, reverse, format) with jsonToShape and shapeUnion.
- Edge cases: empty or whitespace-only Path Data outputs the empty shape, `error` false, and `errorMessage` "". The empty shape is `{ path: "" }`; the Shape layer draws nothing for it. `error` is true exactly when `errorMessage` isn't empty. A Size with a 0 while View Box is in use outputs the empty shape without an error.
- Fill rule: the Shape layer fills with SVG's default nonzero rule. `fill-rule="evenodd"` in a snippet isn't read, so a hole wound the same direction as its outline fills in. Most design tools export holes wound the opposite way, which renders correctly.
- Coordinates: layer-local points of the Shape layer the output drives (`ShapeValue.path`), with the origin at its top-left and y down. The renderer draws the path without clipping it to the layer's size.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values; no special case.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. The output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. A loop of path texts gives a loop of shapes.
- Muted: outputs `null` for Shape, `false` for Error, and `""` for Error Message. The default bypass would copy Path Data into Error Message.
- Hit testing: touches land on the Shape layer's box (its position and size), not on the path. Parts of the path outside that box still draw but don't receive touches.
- Coming from Origami: Origami has no SVG path patch; its custom paths come from JSON to Shape or pasted layers. Importers never produce this patch.

